  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 208 PM CDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\n...Synopsis... \n\nWater vapor indicates several upper waves along or just off the \nwestern CONUS coast. One such upper wave will eject into the Rockies \nand propagate through the western Dakotas, rounding the upper ridge \ninto Canada. This will effectively flatten the upper ridge with \nzonal and eventually northwest flow aloft through the period. \nConfidence is medium to high for this large scale pattern to evolve \ngiven general agreement among ensemble members/systems. \nTemperatures through the period will be near or even slightly \nbelow normal. Shower/storm chances this period will be late \nFriday/Saturday and then again early next week. Surface high \npressure mid-late week should keep the weather mostly dry after \nearly next week. \n \n..Showers/Storms Friday-Saturday... \n\nThe strongest forcing will be west and north of our region, but \nstill anticipate scattered showers/storms within the 'warm sector' \nof the system. That said, most likely scenario indicates upright \ninstability will be very weak to non-existint which would limit\nstorm strength/intensity. Other scenarios/ensemble members do \nbring greater instability to the region, but not many of these \nscenarios exists, and the ones that do are not that much more \nunstable than the most likely scenario. \n\n...Severe Potential Early Next Week...\n\nAs another stronger wave propagates through Canada greater \ninstability will advect into the region, ahead of a cold front. \nMultiple scenarios exist regarding how this will evolve, including \nstrength and timing differences, which will ultimately decide on \nstorm coverage and intensity - severe potential. ML/AI guidance \nindicate a low chance for severe storms Monday-Tuesday, indicating \nthe lower predictability that still exists.",
